Multithreading = Multiple threads executing concurrently.

### Thread Class
```java
class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() { /* code */ }
}
MyThread t = new MyThread();
t.start();
```

### Runnable Interface (Preferred)
```java
class MyRunnable implements Runnable {
    public void run() { /* code */ }
}
Thread t = new Thread(new MyRunnable());
t.start();
```

**Why Runnable better?**
- Java doesn't support multiple inheritance
- More flexible design

**synchronized** prevents thread interference.

### Synchronized Method
```java
public synchronized void increment() {
    count++;
}
```

### Synchronized Block
```java
synchronized(this) {
    count++;
}
```

**How it works:**
- Only ONE thread can execute at a time
- Other threads wait (blocked)

**Use when:** Accessing shared resources (bank account balance, etc.)

**try-with-resources** automatically closes resources (Java 7+).

### Syntax:
```java
try (FileReader fr = new FileReader("file.txt");
     BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(fr)) {
    String line = br.readLine();
} catch (IOException e) {
    e.printStackTrace();
}
// Resources closed automatically!
```

### Benefits:
- No need for finally block
- Resources closed in reverse order
- Works with any AutoCloseable resource

**Before Java 7:**
```java
BufferedReader br = null;
try {
    br = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("file.txt"));
} finally {
    if (br != null) br.close(); // Manual closing
}
```

**Optional** is a container object to avoid NullPointerException.

### Usage:
```java
Optional<String> optional = Optional.ofNullable(getValue());

// Check if present
if (optional.isPresent()) {
    System.out.println(optional.get());
}

// Better: use orElse
String value = optional.orElse("default value");

// or orElseThrow
String value = optional.orElseThrow(() -> new Exception());
```

### Benefits:
- Explicit null handling
- Prevents NullPointerException
- More readable code

**Interview Tip:** Introduced in Java 8 for functional programming

**serialVersionUID** is used during deserialization to verify sender and receiver compatibility.

### Purpose:
- Ensures class version matches during deserialization
- If not declared, JVM generates one automatically
- Mismatched UID throws InvalidClassException

### Usage:
```java
public class Employee implements Serializable {
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;
    private String name;
    private int age;
}
```

**Best Practice:** Always declare explicit serialVersionUID to maintain compatibility across versions.

### @Controller
- Returns **View** (HTML pages)
- Used in MVC pattern with Thymeleaf/JSP
- Need `@ResponseBody` to return data

### @RestController
- `@Controller` + `@ResponseBody` combined
- Returns **JSON/XML** data directly
- Used for REST APIs

**Example**:
```java
@RestController
public class UserAPI {
    @GetMapping("/users")
    public List<User> getUsers() {
        return userService.findAll(); // Returns JSON
    }
}
```

