    const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
    return <div>{count}</div>;
}
```

**Advantages of Functional:**
- Simpler syntax
- Better performance
- Hooks support
- Less boilerplate

**Modern React:** Use functional components with Hooks

**Redux** = State management library for JavaScript apps

### Core Concepts:

**Store:** Single source of truth
```javascript
const store = createStore(reducer);
```

**Action:** Describes what happened
```javascript
{ type: 'ADD_USER', payload: { name: 'John' } }
```

**Reducer:** Specifies state changes
```javascript
function reducer(state = initialState, action) {
    switch(action.type) {
        case 'ADD_USER': return { ...state, users: [...state.users, action.payload] };
        default: return state;
    }
}
```

### When to use:
- Large apps with complex state
- State shared across many components
- State changes frequently

**Don't use:** For simple apps or local component state

### Using fetch:
```javascript
function App() {
    const [data, setData] = useState([]);

    useEffect(() => {
        fetch('https://api.example.com/data')
            .then(res => res.json())
            .then(data => setData(data))
            .catch(err => console.error(err));
    }, []); // Empty array = run once

    return <div>{data.map(item => <p>{item.name}</p>)}</div>;
}
```

### Using axios:
```javascript
import axios from 'axios';

useEffect(() => {
    axios.get('https://api.example.com/data')
        .then(res => setData(res.data));
}, []);
```

**Best Practice:** Handle loading and error states

Two pointers move through array/string to solve problems efficiently.

**Use Cases**:
- Finding pairs with target sum
- Removing duplicates
- Reversing array

**Example** (Find pair with sum = target):
```java
int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;
while (left < right) {
    int sum = arr[left] + arr[right];
    if (sum == target) return true;
    else if (sum < target) left++;
    else right--;
}
```

**Time**: O(n) instead of O(n²) with nested loops

